> ## ⚠️ Safety and Scope Notice
> This is an **educational prototype**. It is **not** a certified fire alarm, **not** a certified fire suppression system, and **not** a substitute for approved fire-protection equipment or emergency procedures. The "suppression" output is a **simulated indicator** (LED). Do not rely on this project to protect people or property.

---

## 1. Project Overview

An Arduino-based prototype that monitors a flame sensor and an MQ-2 smoke/gas sensor, applies filtering and debouncing, warns the user with LEDs and a buzzer, latches an alarm, and activates a **simulated** suppression output.

### Confirmed vs. Proposed Information

| Category | Details |
|---|---|
| **Confirmed project facts** | Arduino-based; involves fire detection; a flame and/or smoke sensor is part of the concept; a buzzer gives the audible alarm; the concept includes an automatic suppression system |
| **Not confirmed** | Original Arduino board, sensor models, wiring, actuator, and suppression components |
| **Proposed in this repository** | Arduino Uno, flame module, MQ-2 module, NPN-driven buzzer, red/green/amber LEDs, reset button, optional LCD/servo/relay, all pin assignments, and all thresholds |

The hardware and wiring in this repository are a **proposed reconstruction**, not the original hardware inventory.

## 2. Problem Statement

Early warning of a possible fire can reduce damage, but certified systems can be costly and opaque for learners. Students need a safe, low-cost platform to study how sensing, signal processing, alarm logic, and automatic response fit together, while understanding why real fire protection requires certified equipment.

## 3. Objectives

- Detect a possible fire condition using suitable low-cost sensors.
- Activate an audible alarm and visual warnings.
- Reduce false alarms using filtering, debouncing, and multi-sensor confirmation.
- Initiate a clearly identified **simulated** suppression response.
- Provide diagnostics, reset logic, and basic fault handling.
- Document the design, testing, and limitations professionally.

## 4. Features

- Dual-sensor monitoring (flame + MQ-2 smoke/gas)
- 8-sample moving-average filter
- Time-based debouncing and hysteresis
- MQ-2 warm-up with automatic clean-air baseline
- State machine: NORMAL → PRE-ALARM → ALARM (latched) → FAULT
- Single-sensor pre-alarm; two-sensor or persistent single-sensor escalation
- Simulated suppression with pre-discharge delay and one-shot duration
- Manual reset (button hold or serial `r`), refused while condition persists
- Heuristic sensor-fault detection (MQ-2 channel)
- Serial diagnostics and **software simulation commands** for safe testing
- Optional I2C LCD and optional servo (compile-time switches)

## 5. Hardware Requirements (Proposed)

**Essential:** Arduino Uno, flame sensor module (analog out), MQ-2 module (analog out), active 5 V buzzer, NPN transistor (2N2222/BC547), 1 kΩ resistor, 3 × 220 Ω resistors, red / green / amber LEDs, push-button, breadboard, jumper wires, USB cable.

**Optional:** 16x2 I2C LCD, micro servo with external 5 V supply, opto-isolated relay module (low-voltage demo load only).

