## 13. Limitations

- Not certified; no approval to any fire-detection standard.
- Low-cost sensors have limited accuracy, drift, and cross-sensitivity.
- Flame sensor needs line of sight and is affected by sunlight and artificial IR sources.
- MQ-2 needs preheat and calibration; it is not a smoke-particle detector.
- Fixed thresholds do not adapt to environment (no automatic baseline tracking).
- Fault detection is heuristic; a disconnected sensor may not be detected.
- No hardware watchdog; alarm latch is stored in RAM only and is lost on power failure.
- Single microcontroller is a single point of failure; no supervised wiring or backup power.
- The suppression output is simulated and releases nothing.

## 14. Safety Precautions

- Never test with real fire or flammable materials near the circuit.
- Use only low-voltage supplies (USB/5 V) for the demonstration.
- Do **not** connect this prototype, its GPIO pins, or an unverified relay to mains-powered equipment, pumps, pressurized systems, valves, or extinguishing agents.
- A physical suppression actuator requires rated drivers and isolation, independent power, abort and manual override, interlocks, supervised wiring, safe agent selection, standards compliance, and professional review.
- Keep the MQ-2 away from ignition sources; its heater becomes hot.
- Power the servo from an external supply with common ground.
- Always rely on certified detectors and established emergency procedures for real protection.

